Configuration :

EC2 configuration (required)

Instance type: t3.large (2 vCPU, 8 GB RAM)

AMI: Amazon Linux 2023 (recommended; defaults to XFS filesystem)

Root EBS volume: 32 GB (minimum)

Security Group: Inbound SSH (22) from your IP

(Ollama API listens on 127.0.0.1:11434 by default, so no extra inbound ports needed)


1) Connect & update OS
# SSH into the instance
ssh -i <your-key>.pem ec2-user@<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>

# Update packages
sudo yum update -y

2) Install prerequisites
sudo yum install -y curl git

3) Install Ollama (CPU mode on t3.large)
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh


This:

Creates the ollama user & systemd service

Starts the API on 127.0.0.1:11434

Warns “No GPU detected” (expected on t3.large)

Verify service/port:

systemctl status ollama
ss -ltnp | grep 11434


Expected: a LISTEN line on 127.0.0.1:11434.

If you see Error: ... bind: address already in use
→ Service is already running. Just:

sudo systemctl restart ollama

4) (If needed) Expand disk from 8 GB → 32 GB

If you started with 8 GB and hit “no space left on device”:

4.1 Modify the volume in AWS Console

EC2 → Volumes → select root volume → Modify volume → set 32 GiB → Modify.

4.2 Grow partition & filesystem on the instance (Amazon Linux 2023 uses XFS):

# Grow partition 1 on the NVMe disk
sudo yum install -y cloud-utils-growpart || true   # if space is tight, do this step after volume grows
sudo growpart /dev/nvme0n1 1

# Grow XFS filesystem for root (/)
sudo xfs_growfs -d /

# Confirm new size
df -h


Expected / around 32G.

5) Pull a code-capable model (Code Llama 7B)
ollama pull codellama:7b


Check:

ollama list


You should see codellama:7b (~3.8 GB).

6) Generate a Linux shell script with AI

Example prompt (disk usage alert):

ollama run codellama:7b "Write a bash script that checks disk usage of /, removes the % symbol, compares usage > 80, and saves the script ready to run"


7) Create the working disk_alert.sh (fixed parsing)


nano disk_alert.sh


Paste:

#!/bin/bash

THRESHOLD=80

# Extract only the numeric percentage for root filesystem
USAGE=$(df -h / | awk 'NR==2 {gsub("%","",$5); print $5}')

if [ "$USAGE" -gt "$THRESHOLD" ]; then
  echo "⚠ Disk usage is above $THRESHOLD%. Current: $USAGE%"
else
  echo " Disk usage is under control. Current: $USAGE%"
fi


Save, make executable, run:

chmod +x disk_alert.sh
./disk_alert.sh


Example output:

-- Disk usage is under control. Current: 28%
